</style>
</head>
<body>
<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="kicker">ACM Mid-Southeast Conference &middot; November 2026</div>
    <h1>Does Speculative Decoding Theory Hold on Consumer-Grade Hardware?</h1>
    <p class="lede">A small model guesses, a large model checks. We tested the published speedup formula on an Apple Silicon workstation with 480 benchmark prompts and compared it with what the theory predicts.</p>
    <p class="by">David Frazier &middot; Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, UVA Wise</p>
    <div class="btns">
      <a class="btn primary" href="https://github.com/def3qu/SpeculativeDecoding">Code on GitHub</a>
      <a class="btn" href="#try">Try it yourself</a>
      <a class="btn" href="#results">See the results</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<nav><div class="wrap sans">
  <a href="#problem">The problem</a><a href="#idea">The idea</a><a href="#theory">The theory</a><a href="#results">Results</a><a href="#holds">Does it hold?</a><a href="#try">Try it</a><a href="#limits">Limits</a><a href="#refs">References</a>
</div></nav>

<main class="wrap">

<section id="problem">
  <h2>The problem: one token at a time</h2>
  <p>Large language models (LLMs) write text one token at a time, and every new token needs a pass through the whole model. Commercial services raise concerns about privacy, cost per token and energy use, and running open-weight models locally is one alternative. But local models often run slowly.</p>
  <p>The reason is memory. Each token requires reading every model weight from memory, about 16 GB for an 8-billion-parameter model at 16-bit precision. A processor does roughly one arithmetic operation per byte read, so it spends most of each pass waiting for data. Single-user inference is limited by memory bandwidth, not computation. Datacenters hide this by batching many users together, and a personal machine serves only one.</p>
  <p>That leaves compute idle. If checking several tokens costs about the same as producing one, can we get several useful tokens out of each pass over the weights?</p>
</section>

<section id="idea">
  <h2>The idea: draft, verify, keep</h2>
  <p>Speculative decoding uses two models that share a tokenizer. A small, fast <b>draft</b> model guesses the next <i>K</i> tokens. The large <b>target</b> model then checks all of them in a single pass. Guesses that match what the target would have written are kept, and the first wrong guess is replaced by the target's own token.</p>
  <div class="flow">
    <div class="step"><b>1. Draft guesses</b>Small model proposes K tokens, for example <i>Paris , known for</i></div>
    <div class="step ok"><b>2. Target checks</b>Large model scores all K guesses in one pass</div>
    <div class="step warn"><b>3. Keep and fix</b>Accepted tokens are kept; the first rejection is replaced by the target's own token</div>
  </div>
  <p>With greedy decoding (always taking the most likely token), the text is identical to what the large model would produce alone. The method changes the speed, not the answer. In float16 arithmetic, rounding differences flipped a near-tie in 1.1% of our runs, which is a numerical effect and not a flaw in the algorithm.</p>
</section>

<section id="theory">
  <h2>The theory: a speedup formula</h2>
  <p>Leviathan et al. (2023) give an analytic model for the expected speedup:</p>
  <div class="formula">
    Speedup =
    <span class="frac"><span class="top">1 &minus; <i>&alpha;</i><sup>(<i>K</i>+1)</sup></span><span>(1 &minus; <i>&alpha;</i>)(<i>Kc</i> + 1)</span></span>
    <div class="legend"><b><i>&alpha;</i></b> acceptance rate: chance the target accepts a draft token &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <b><i>K</i></b> draft length: tokens guessed per round &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <b><i>c</i></b> cost ratio: draft pass time divided by target pass time</div>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">Longer drafts produce more tokens per round but cost more draft passes, so the speedup rises and then falls as <i>K</i> grows. The theory has been validated mainly on datacenter hardware. We asked whether it holds on a consumer-class machine.</p>
</section>

<section id="results">
  <h2>Results</h2>
  <p><b>Setup.</b> Apple Silicon Mac Studio (PyTorch on MPS), Llama 3.2 1B as the draft and Llama 3.1 8B as the target, float16, greedy decoding. SpecBench: 480 prompts across six tasks (MT-Bench, translation, summarization, question answering, math, retrieval-augmented generation), 80 prompts each, up to 100 new tokens, K = 1, 2, 3, 5 and 8, one run per configuration.</p>
  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="card"><div class="big">1.86x</div><div class="cap">Overall speedup, best at K = 3 to 5</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="big">2.14x</div><div class="cap">Math at K = 5, the best case</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="big">1.63x</div><div class="cap">Translation at K = 3, the lowest peak</div></div>
  </div>
  <h3>Measured speedup by draft length</h3>
