</svg>
  <p class="note">Speedup is speculative tokens per second divided by baseline tokens per second, aggregated over 80 prompts per task.</p>

  <h3>Acceptance rate and cost ratio by task</h3>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <tr><th>Task</th><th class="n">Alpha</th><th class="n">c</th><th class="n">Best K</th><th class="n">Best speedup</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Math reasoning</td><td class="n">0.88</td><td class="n">0.21</td><td class="n">5</td><td class="n">2.14x</td></tr>
    <tr><td>MT-Bench (multi-turn conversations)</td><td class="n">0.82</td><td class="n">0.21</td><td class="n">3</td><td class="n">1.83x</td></tr>
    <tr><td>RAG (retrieval-augmented generation)</td><td class="n">0.80</td><td class="n">0.18</td><td class="n">3 or 5</td><td class="n">1.92x</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Question answering</td><td class="n">0.80</td><td class="n">0.23</td><td class="n">3</td><td class="n">1.72x</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Summarization</td><td class="n">0.79</td><td class="n">0.18</td><td class="n">3</td><td class="n">1.85x</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Translation</td><td class="n">0.73</td><td class="n">0.22</td><td class="n">3</td><td class="n">1.63x</td></tr>
  </table></div>
  <p class="note">Alpha is estimated from the K = 1 runs. Predictable text, such as step-by-step arithmetic, accepts more guesses and rewards longer drafts. In translation the small model often chooses a different but valid wording, so long drafts are wasted.</p>
  <p>The draft model is about 6.5 times smaller than the target but only about 4 to 5 times faster per pass (c of 0.18 to 0.23), so small models do not benefit fully from their size on this hardware.</p>
</section>

<section id="holds">
  <h2>Does the theory hold?</h2>
  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="card"><div class="big">1.9%</div><div class="cap">Mean error predicting K = 2 to 8 speedups from alpha measured at K = 1 (worst case 7.5%)</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="big">5.3%</div><div class="cap">Extra time for a target pass that checks 9 tokens versus producing 1, consistent with a memory-bound machine</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="big">29%</div><div class="cap">Worst over-prediction (translation) when one global alpha is used for every task</div></div>
  </div>
  <p><b>Largely yes, with a condition.</b> When alpha is estimated per task, the formula predicts the measured speedups closely. A single average alpha is not enough: acceptance varies from about 0.73 to 0.88 across tasks, and ignoring that over-predicts by 8.7% on average. The geometric model of acceptance fits for most tasks; translation is the exception, with an implied alpha that rises as K grows.</p>
</section>

<section id="try">
  <h2>Try it yourself</h2>
  <p>Three programs in the repository let you run speculative decoding on your own computer. They need Python 3.9 or newer, and a GPU (NVIDIA or Apple Silicon) is strongly recommended. The first run downloads the models.</p>
<pre><code>git clone https://github.com/def3qu/SpeculativeDecoding
cd SpeculativeDecoding
pip install torch transformers</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><b>speculative_playground.py</b> <span class="tag">compare</span> answers a prompt with the large model alone and again with speculative decoding, then prints the measured alpha, c and the speedup the formula predicts. Type <code>:sweep</code> to see K = 1 to 8 in one run.</li>
    <li><b>speculative_answer.py</b> <span class="tag">chat</span> answers with speculative decoding only (K = 3), using Qwen2.5 0.5B to draft for Qwen2.5 7B. About 16 GB of memory.</li>
    <li><b>speculative_web.py</b> <span class="tag">browser</span> the same chat as a web page at 127.0.0.1:8000. Needs <code>speculative_answer.py</code> in the same folder.</li>
  </ul>
<pre><code>python speculative_playground.py
python speculative_playground.py --pair tiny      # about 4 GB, for small machines
python speculative_answer.py
python speculative_web.py</code></pre>
  <p class="note">The draft and target must come from the same model family so they share a tokenizer. These programs use a KV cache for both models; the research benchmark does not, so absolute speeds differ.</p>
</section>

<section id="limits">
  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>One machine and one model family.</b> Results come from a single Apple Silicon workstation and one Llama draft and target pair.</li>
    <li><b>No KV cache in the benchmark.</b> Both the baseline and speculative runs recompute the full sequence at each step, so absolute speeds are lower than in production engines, and the cost ratio and verification cost could differ with caching. The speedup ratio compares like with like.</li>
    <li><b>One run per configuration.</b> Timing noise is averaged across 80 prompts per task rather than across repetitions.</li>
    <li><b>Output is not always byte-identical.</b> 27 of 2,400 speculative runs (1.1%) differed from the baseline text because of float16 rounding.</li>
    <li><b>Greedy decoding only.</b> Sampling at a nonzero temperature was not tested.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="refs">
  <h2>References</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Y. Leviathan, M. Kalman, Y. Matias. <i>Fast Inference from Transformers via Speculative Decoding.</i> ICML 2023.</li>
    <li>SpecBench benchmark prompts: six task types, 80 prompts each.</li>
    <li>Models: Llama 3.2 1B Instruct and Llama 3.1 8B Instruct (Meta); Qwen2.5 Instruct family (Alibaba).</li>
  </ul>
</section>

